course: dictionary
lesson: 12734
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is meant. The word driven is a good example. «She was driven to school by her dad» means someone else took her in a car. She did not steer the car herself.
explain: «He drives the bus every morning» uses drive, which means to steer a vehicle. Its past form is drove, but driven comes after helper words such as «has» or «was»: «The bus has been driven around the lake.» Here the bus is the thing being steered.
explain: Driven can also be an adjective. «Maya is a driven runner» means she works very hard to reach her goal. The clues are the words around it: training every day and wanting to win. To check, swap in the meaning, "very keen to succeed", and see if the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Driven can mean pushed or moved by a force. «A wind-driven mill» is a mill turned by the wind, not by a person. Electricity and water can also drive machines, so the rest of the sentence tells you what is doing the pushing.
fact: Driven is a form of the verb drive: drive, drives, drove, driven, driving.
fact: «Driven» can mean taken somewhere in a car, bus or train: «She was driven to school.»
fact: «Driven» can mean pushed or moved by a force, such as wind, water or electricity: «A wind-driven mill.»
fact: A driven person is very keen to succeed and works hard to reach a goal.
fact: Distance driven can be worked out with speed × time: 60 miles per hour for 2 hours is 120 miles.
exercise: «The bus has been driven around the town all morning.» What does driven mean here? | *Steered by a driver in a vehicle | Painted bright red | Stopped for a long rest
exercise: «Sam is a driven swimmer who trains before school every day.» What does driven mean? | *Very keen to succeed | Taken somewhere by car | Pushed by the wind
exercise: «The old mill was driven by the river.» What was the mill driven by? | A team of horses | A broken bicycle | Money from the town | *Water from the river
test: Which sentence uses driven to mean taken somewhere in a car? | *Grandad was driven to the station by Aunt Jo | The ball was kicked over the fence | The paint was driven into the wall | The kettle was boiled for tea
test: What is the best way to check the meaning of a hard word in a sentence? | *Swap in your meaning and see if the sentence still makes sense | Skip it and never come back | Guess the longest word | Look only at the first word
exam: Which form of drive completes this sentence? «The car has been ___ carefully for years.» | driving | drives | *driven | drove
